.Dd November 19, 2026
.Dt MOUNT 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mount ,
.Nm umount2
.Nd attach or detach a file system
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/mount.h
.Ft int
.Fn mount "const char *source" "const char *target" "const char *type" "unsigned long flags" "const void *data"
.Ft int
.Fn umount2 "const char *target" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn mount
attaches the file system on
.Fa source
at the directory
.Fa target .
.Fa type
selects the file-system type,
.Fa flags
supplies mount flags, and
.Fa data
points to a null-terminated option string.
A null
.Fa data
is treated as an empty string.
Both calls require effective user ID 0, except as
.Er EPERM
under ERRORS says.
.Pp
The supported types are
.Ql vfat ,
.Ql exfat ,
.Ql ext2 ,
.Ql krffs ,
.Ql tmpfs ,
.Ql mqueue ,
.Ql debugfs ,
.Ql pstore ,
and
.Ql devpts ,
which shows the system's own
.Pa /dev/pts
wherever it is mounted, since Linux's option
.Ql newinstance
is not honoured.
.Dv MS_RDONLY ,
.Dv MS_NOSUID ,
.Dv MS_NOEXEC ,
and
.Dv MS_NODEV
are enforced;
.Dv MS_NODEV
makes an open of a device node on the mount return
.Er EACCES .
.Dv MS_SYNCHRONOUS
and
.Dv MS_DIRSYNC
make ext2 put each metadata write on the medium before the next, with a
flush of the disk's cache, in an order that a stop between two writes
leaves repairable without losing a file's data; without them ext2 writes
back as Linux's does.
A change of a directory's entries, and a block a directory or a symbolic
link takes, are written to the device during the call that makes them, as
is the directory's inode where the call's next step depends on it, such
as a rename's new name in a block the directory just took: the device has
answered each write before the call goes on, but the disk's cache is not
flushed, so a write may still be in it.
Anything else an operation changes stays in the buffer cache until
.Xr sync 2 ,
.Xr fsync 2 ,
unmount, the cache's eviction, or the kernel's write-back, which comes
once the data has been dirty for
.Pa /proc/sys/vm/dirty_expire_centisecs
or the dirty buffers pass the thresholds
.Xr proc 5
gives, and a stop before then leaves what
.Xr e2fsck 8
repairs.
.Dv MS_NOATIME ,
.Dv MS_NODIRATIME ,
.Dv MS_RELATIME ,
and
.Dv MS_SILENT
are recorded in the mount table.
.Dv MS_STRICTATIME
takes
.Dv MS_NOATIME
and
.Dv MS_RELATIME
off the flags given, as on Linux, and is not recorded.
.Dv MS_REMOUNT
gives the mount whose root
.Fa target
names the other flags, keeping its
.Dv MS_NOATIME ,
.Dv MS_NODIRATIME
and
.Dv MS_RELATIME
when none of them or
.Dv MS_STRICTATIME
is given, as on Linux, reads neither
.Fa source
nor
.Fa filesystemtype ,
and gives
.Fa data
to the file system, of whose options tmpfs takes
.Ql size=
and
.Ql nr_inodes= ,
and ext2 the options it takes at a mount, below.
On ext2 it makes the mount read-only, synced and marked clean as an
unmount leaves it, or writable, checked as a writable mount is; tmpfs
changes between the two at once; every other type refuses a change
between them with
.Er EINVAL
and takes one of the other flags.
A mount is not made read-only while a description is open on it for
writing, one a shared mapping alone holds among them, or on ext2 while a
file removed from it is open,
.Er EBUSY ,
and an open for writing during the change is
.Er EROFS ,
as on Linux.
Before the change the pages that shared mappings stored into are written
to the files, as Linux syncs the file system first; a write that fails is
the call's error, and the mount stays writable with the pages kept for a
later write.
A mount is not made writable on a device that refuses writes or has failed
one, or on ext2 when the volume has a feature the driver reads but does
not write, which mounted it read-only, or when the volume's check stops,
.Er EROFS .
A remount that adds
.Dv MS_SYNCHRONOUS
or
.Dv MS_DIRSYNC
to a writable ext2 mount first writes out everything the mount has
pending, holding new changes off, as Linux syncs the file system before a
remount, so that the order the mount wrote back in still holds at a stop;
a write that fails is the call's error, and the flags stay as they were.
With
.Dv MS_BIND
as well, the change is to the named mount's flags, including on an
original mount.
Its filesystem keeps its read-only and write-ordering
flags.
Only descriptions opened through that mount make it
.Er EBUSY .
Clearing the mount's read-only flag still leaves a read-only filesystem
read-only.
Without
.Dv MS_BIND ,
the filesystem changes through every mount that shows it.
Only the
named mount takes the per-mount flags.
A remount that supplies no atime flag keeps the named mount's atime flags.
Other bits, including
.Dv MS_MOVE ,
are refused.
.Pp
.Dv MS_BIND
shows the directory or file
.Fa source
names, a path, at the directory or file
.Fa target
names, with the flags of the mount the source is on;
.Fa filesystemtype
and
.Fa data
are not read, and other flags are ignored, as on Linux.
The two are one file system: a file has one inode, its locks, its shared
mappings and its watches whichever path names it, and
.Xr stat 2
gives it one device.
With
.Dv MS_REC ,
the mounts below the source are shown below the new mount too, each a bind
of its own, so a bind of
.Pa /dev
has
.Pa /dev/shm .
The target must be a directory for a directory and a file for a file,
.Er ENOTDIR .
A place inside
.Pa /dev ,
.Pa /proc
or
.Pa /sys
may be the source, as bubblewrap binds
.Pa /proc/sys
read-only over itself, but not
.Pa /proc/PID/fd/N ,
.Er EINVAL .
Inside them a bind's target is the root of a mount or a name in the root
directory of one, such as
.Pa /proc/sys ,
which the new mount is entered by; a place deeper than that is
.Er EINVAL .
A file of
.Pa /proc
opened for writing through a read-only mount is
.Er EROFS .
Both are recorded by the paths they resolve to, so that a bind over a
symbolic link covers the file the link names.
A bind of a directory of KRFFS, whose files move as they grow, moves with
them, and the directory a bind shows is busy to
.Xr rmdir 2 ,
.Er EBUSY ,
until the bind is unmounted.
Through a bind of the KRFFS boot root a file's mode, owner and times do
not change,
.Er EROFS ,
since the root changes them only through its own paths.
The mount the source is on cannot be unmounted while a bind of it stands,
.Er EBUSY ;
a bind is unmounted as any mount is, and the mounts below it first.
.Pa /proc/self/mountinfo
gives a bind the device of the mount it shows and, as its root, the path
of its source within that file system.
A change of propagation alone,
.Dv MS_PRIVATE ,
.Dv MS_SLAVE ,
.Dv MS_SHARED
or
.Dv MS_UNBINDABLE ,
with
.Dv MS_REC
or without, succeeds and changes nothing, since a mount is seen in the
namespaces that had it when it was made and in none it is made in later.
.Pp
A mount is made in the caller's mount namespace alone, and an unmount
removes it from that namespace, and from the system when no other
namespace has it; see
.Xr unshare 2 .
See
.Xr mount 8
for the command interface.
.Pp
For
.Ql tmpfs ,
a file system in memory whose files go at the unmount, the source is
any string, conventionally
.Ql none
or
.Ql tmpfs ,
and is recorded as given; nothing is claimed.
Its root has mode 1777, or the octal mode an option
.Ql mode=
gives.
Its files hold at most the pages the option
.Ql size=
allows: bytes, with
.Ql k ,
.Ql m
or
.Ql g
after them, or a percentage of the machine's memory with
.Ql % ,
rounded up to whole pages; a write past it fails with
.Er ENOSPC ,
.Ql size=0
is no limit, and without the option the limit is half the machine's
memory, as on Linux.
.Xr statfs 2
gives the limit as the blocks.
It has at most the inodes the option
.Ql nr_inodes=
gives, the root's among them: a count, with
.Ql k ,
.Ql m ,
.Ql g ,
.Ql t ,
.Ql p
or
.Ql e
after it for a power of 1024; each node takes one, and so does each name
of a file past its first, and an extended attribute 40 bytes and its name
and value of the 1024 bytes each inode stands for, as on Linux.
A node, name or attribute past the limit fails with
.Er ENOSPC ,
.Ql nr_inodes=0
is no limit, and without the option the limit is half the machine's
pages.
.Xr statfs 2
gives it as the inodes.
A value of either option that is no number fails the mount with
.Er EINVAL .
The other options Linux's tmpfs takes are accepted and change nothing.
A remount takes a new
.Ql size=
and
.Ql nr_inodes= ,
and changes neither and fails with
.Er EINVAL
when either is smaller than what the files hold, or sets a limit on a
mount that has none, as on Linux; 0 lifts its limit for good.
A file in memory reaches 16 TiB less a page on both architectures, as on
Linux on i386; Linux on x86-64 allows more.
.Pp
For
.Ql devpts ,
the source is any string, recorded as given, and nothing is claimed.
Every mount shows the system's one set of terminals.
It takes Linux's options:
.Ql uid=
and
.Ql gid= ,
the owner and group of a new terminal, numbers the caller's user
namespace maps, without which it is the opener's;
.Ql mode= ,
in octal, its permission bits, 0600 by default;
.Ql ptmxmode= ,
in octal, 0000 by default, which is shown and leaves
.Pa /dev/pts/ptmx
with the mode of
.Pa /dev/ptmx ;
.Ql max= ,
from 0 to 1048576, below which a new terminal's number must be, or
.Pa /dev/ptmx
fails with
.Er ENOSPC ;
and
.Ql newinstance ,
which every mount is.
A terminal opened through
.Pa /dev/ptmx
takes the options of the devpts mount at
.Pa /dev/pts
under the opener's root, and without one the opener's user, group 5 and
mode 0620.
A remount takes its options from the defaults again, as on Linux.
Another option or a bad number is
.Er EINVAL .
.Pp
For
.Ql mqueue ,
the source is any string, recorded as given, nothing is claimed, and the
options are ignored.
The mount lists the queues of the caller's IPC namespace; see
.Xr mq_open 2 .
.Pp
For
.Ql debugfs ,
the source is any string, recorded as given, and nothing is claimed.
Every mount shows the one tree of files the kernel registers, as on
Linux, and the options
.Ql mode= ,
.Ql uid=
and
.Ql gid=
set its root directory's mode, in octal, and owner; see
.Xr debugfs 5 .
.Pp
For
.Ql pstore ,
the source is any string, recorded as given, nothing is claimed, and the
options are ignored; every mount shows the records the last boot left, as
on Linux; see
.Xr pstore 5 .
.Pp
For every other type, the source must name a published block device
under
.Pa /dev :
a disk or partition containing a volume,
.Pa /dev/bootpart ,
or an attached loop device.
The source string is used directly; a relative source is not resolved
against the working directory.
A read-only source makes the mount read-only regardless of
.Fa flags .
.Ss Argument checks and mounting
The handler checks for a current process and privilege before copying
any strings.
It copies the source, then the target, makes the target absolute,
then copies the type, refuses a type the caller may not mount with
.Er EPERM ,
and copies the options.
The source, target, type, and options must fit buffers of 64, 4096, 16,
and 128 bytes respectively, including the terminating null byte.
A relative target is joined to the current working directory, and an
empty one is
.Er ENOENT .
Repeated slashes and dot components are removed;
.Ql ..
is resolved by walking the parent directories.
.Pp
The VFS then performs these checks in order:
.Bl -enum
.It
Recognize the type, or return
.Er ENODEV .
.It
Reject unsupported flag bits with
.Er EINVAL .
.It
Resolve and hold the target, returning any path-resolution error.
.It
For a mount of type
.Ql proc ,
use the mounter's active PID namespace.
A new proc mount may cover the system's
.Pa /proc .
An inherited mount keeps its original PID namespace.
.It
Require the absolute target path to fit 255 bytes, excluding the null
byte, or return
.Er ENAMETOOLONG .
.It
Reject the root of the mount table and a directory of the system's
synthetic
.Pa /dev ,
.Pa /proc
or
.Pa /sys
themselves with
.Er EINVAL ;
a directory of the tmpfs
.Pa /dev/shm
below them is a target like any other, and so is the root of a bind of
one of them, as a sandbox mounts a tmpfs over its own
.Pa /dev .
.It
Take the root of a mount already placed as a target like any other
directory: the new mount stacks on it, as on Linux, and a path through
the target reaches the mount placed last.
.It
Read the target's attributes and require a directory, or return
.Er ENOTDIR .
.It
Rebuild the target's path from the root of the mount table, the path
the mount table records whatever path named the target, a root set by
.Xr chroot 2
included, within the same 255-byte limit, or return
.Er ENAMETOOLONG .
.It
Under the parent directory's namespace lock, reject a covered or
reserved target with
.Er EBUSY .
.El
.Pp
The kernel then resolves the source device and claims its sector range.
An overlapping mount or write-capable raw descriptor prevents the
claim, even when another device name denotes the same sectors.
A loop device is identified by the device itself and permits one mount.
The claim remains until unmount.
.Pp
The mount table grows from eight slots as needed.
The kernel reserves a slot before running the file-system constructor,
holds the covered directory, and keeps a reference to its parent mount.
The mount becomes active only after the constructor succeeds.
A failure releases the claim and references acquired by the call.
.Pp
The kernel makes eight mounts at boot, and they are permanent: the root;
the synthetic
.Pa /dev
and
.Pa /proc ;
a tmpfs at
.Pa /dev/shm ,
with
.Dv MS_NOSUID
and
.Dv MS_NODEV ;
the tmpfs that holds the files of
.Xr memfd_create 2 ,
which has no path;
the synthetic
.Pa /sys ,
with
.Dv MS_RDONLY ,
.Dv MS_NOSUID ,
.Dv MS_NODEV
and
.Dv MS_NOEXEC ;
and an
.Ql mqueue
mount at
.Pa /dev/mqueue ,
with
.Dv MS_NOSUID ,
.Dv MS_NODEV
and
.Dv MS_NOEXEC ,
which lists the queues of the first IPC namespace;
and debugfs at
.Pa /sys/kernel/debug ,
with
.Dv MS_NOSUID ,
.Dv MS_NODEV
and
.Dv MS_NOEXEC .
On Linux the init system makes most of these, and each it makes may be
unmounted.
With the
.Cm ramoops.
options the kernel also mounts pstore at
.Pa /sys/fs/pstore ,
with the same three flags, as systemd does on Linux; that mount is not
permanent.
.Pa /dev
is synthetic and has no directory to cover, so
.Pa /dev/shm
and
.Pa /dev/mqueue
stand in its root directory by name, as
.Pa /dev
stands in the root's, and debugfs and pstore stand in the root directory of
.Pa /sys
by the names
.Pa kernel/debug
and
.Pa fs/pstore ,
whose
.Pa ..
are
.Pa /sys/kernel
and
.Pa /sys/fs ;
a mount may be placed on a directory inside
.Pa /dev/shm .
.Pp
An active mount's source, target and type appear in
.Pa /proc/mounts
with
.Ql ro
or
.Ql rw
and whichever of
.Ql sync ,
.Ql dirsync ,
.Ql nosuid ,
.Ql nodev ,
.Ql noexec ,
.Ql noatime ,
.Ql nodiratime
and
.Ql relatime
are set, as
.Xr proc 5
describes; the silent flag and the file system's own options are not
shown.
KRFFS, mqueue and pstore ignore the option string; FAT32, exFAT,
tmpfs and debugfs parse it.
.Pp
vfat and exfat take Linux's options.
Both take
.Ql uid=
and
.Ql gid= ,
the owner and group of every entry, which the caller's user namespace
maps;
.Ql fmask=
and
.Ql dmask= ,
in octal, the permission bits files and directories lack, and
.Ql umask= ,
both at once, all three the caller's umask by default and the owner and
group the caller's real ones;
.Ql allow_utime= ,
whose 0020 and 0002 let the file's group and others set its times to
given values, by default those of the two that
.Ql dmask
leaves; the error policy
.Ql errors=continue ,
.Ql errors=panic
or
.Ql errors=remount-ro ;
.Ql iocharset=utf8 ,
the only character set;
.Ql time_offset= ,
the minutes east of UTC, at most 1440 either way, that stamps are in;
and
.Ql discard .
vfat also takes
.Ql showexec ,
under which a file's execute bits are kept for the extensions EXE, COM and
BAT alone;
.Ql rodir ,
under which a directory keeps the read-only attribute, which it otherwise
ignores;
.Ql quiet ,
under which a change of owner or group, or a set-id or sticky bit, that
chown or chmod refuses succeeds and changes nothing;
.Ql tz=UTC ,
a time offset of 0; without either, the stamps are in the zone
.Xr settimeofday 2
set;
.Ql nfs=nostale_ro ,
which mounts read-only;
.Ql codepage=437 ,
the only code page; and
.Ql check= ,
.Ql shortname= ,
.Ql utf8 ,
.Ql uni_xlate ,
.Ql nonumtail ,
.Ql usefree ,
.Ql nocase ,
.Ql debug ,
.Ql sys_immutable ,
.Ql flush ,
.Ql nfs ,
.Ql dos1xfloppy ,
and the deprecated
.Ql conv= ,
.Ql fat= ,
.Ql blocksize= ,
.Ql cvf_format= ,
.Ql cvf_options=
and
.Ql posix ,
which are kept and shown and change nothing else.
exfat also takes
.Ql sys_tz ,
under which a stamp without a valid UTC offset is read in the zone
.Xr settimeofday 2
set, as without it a stamp is read in
.Ql time_offset ;
.Ql keep_last_dots
and
.Ql zero_size_dir ,
which are kept and shown; and the deprecated
.Ql utf8 ,
.Ql debug ,
.Ql namecase=
and
.Ql codepage= .
A deprecated option logs
.Ql TYPE: Deprecated parameter 'NAME' .
Another option, a bad value, or another code page or character set is
.Er EINVAL .
The mount table shows the options as Linux's
.Fn fat_show_options
and
.Fn exfat_show_options
do.
ext2 takes Linux's options:
.Ql errors=continue ,
.Ql errors=remount-ro
and
.Ql errors=panic ,
which override the volume's own policy;
.Ql minixdf
and
.Ql bsddf ,
whether
.Xr statfs 2
counts the volume's own blocks;
.Ql grpid
or
.Ql bsdgroups ,
under which a new file takes its directory's group, and
.Ql nogrpid
or
.Ql sysvgroups ;
.Ql resuid=
and
.Ql resgid= ,
the user and group besides a caller with
.Dv CAP_SYS_RESOURCE
who may take the reserved blocks;
.Ql sb= ,
first in the string, the superblock to mount from in units of 1024
bytes, whose group descriptors follow it;
.Ql nouid32 ,
which keeps 16 bits of an owner on the medium and reads no more;
.Ql user_xattr
and
.Ql nouser_xattr ,
without which a
.Ql user.
attribute is
.Er EOPNOTSUPP ;
and
.Ql acl ,
.Ql noacl ,
.Ql debug ,
.Ql oldalloc ,
.Ql orlov ,
.Ql nobh ,
.Ql quota ,
.Ql usrquota ,
.Ql grpquota ,
.Ql noquota ,
.Ql reservation ,
.Ql noreservation ,
.Ql xip
and
.Ql dax ,
which are kept and shown and change nothing else, with
.Ql dax
turned off as on a device without DAX.
The volume's
.Ql s_default_mount_opts ,
.Ql s_def_resuid
and
.Ql s_def_resgid
give the defaults, and a remount starts from the options in force.
Another option, or a malformed number, is
.Er EINVAL .
.Ss Dirty-volume checks
Before a writable mount, FAT32 checks a dirty volume with limits of
32 directory levels including the root and 16 surplus tails.
Duplicate-name comparison requires each ordinary short entry to lie
within the first 4096 directory slots; long-name and deleted slots
count toward that position.
Exceeding any bound makes the mount read-only and logs the reason.
.Pp
KRFFS repair rebuilds the backward links, puts a file a stopped rename
left in no directory back in one, frees nameless nodes and resolves a
name on two nodes on any dirty volume, in passes when the volume has
more named nodes than its index of 262144 holds.
A volume the kernel has no memory to index, or with more nodes of one
name hash than the index holds, mounts read-only after the rest of the
repair, logs that
.Ql fsck.krffs
is owed, and keeps its dirty flag, since a write could act on one of
two nodes of one name.
A read-only mount skips repair.
A KRFFS volume whose core cannot undo a failed move halts: the file system
turns read-only through every mount of it until the next mount repairs it,
as ext4 does after an error, with each mount's own flag left as it was,
and its dirty flag stays set.
.Pp
An ext2 volume with its valid bit clear or error bit set is checked
before a writable mount or remount.
A check that ends repairs the bits of blocks and inodes files still name,
clears an entry naming an unused inode and sets an entry's type to its
inode's, as
.Ql e2fsck -p
does after a create a power cut stopped,
keeps what only e2fsck can repair, and leaves the mount writable.
A check that stops, at a structural fault, which sets the error bit, or at
a limit of its own, which leaves the volume's state as it was, leaves the
mount read-only and logs
.Ql ext2: SOURCE: read-only with errors, run e2fsck
or
.Ql ext2: SOURCE: read-only unchecked, run e2fsck ;
a remount writable checks the volume again and is
.Er EROFS
while the check still stops.
A fault an ext2 mount meets later sets the error bit on a writable
mount and then, as Linux's
.Ql errors=
says, continues, makes the mount read-only, or stops the kernel.
A mount made read-only by a fault admits no new change and allocates
nothing; the operations already under way finish or undo their changes,
the file data it accepted before is written, and a remount writable
checks the volume again.
A remount without
.Ql errors=
keeps the policy in force.
.Ss Unmounting
.Fn umount2
requires
.Fa target
to resolve to the root of a mount.
A target that ends in a slash, or whose last component is
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&.. ,
must reach a directory, so a file named with a trailing slash returns
.Er ENOTDIR ,
as on Linux, where the same file named without one returns
.Er EINVAL .
.Fa flags
must be zero or
.Dv MNT_DETACH ;
.Dv MNT_FORCE ,
.Dv UMOUNT_NOFOLLOW ,
and all other values return
.Er EINVAL .
Without
.Dv MNT_DETACH
the seven permanent mounts return
.Er EBUSY .
.Pp
.Dv MNT_DETACH
takes the mount, and every mount below it, from the caller's mount
namespace at once, however busy they are: no path of the namespace
reaches them, and they leave
.Pa /proc/self/mountinfo ,
while a descriptor or a working directory on one of them still works.
The caller's root and the mounts below it stay, so that after
.Xr pivot_root 2
the old root can be let go of while the new one, which was below it, is
in use.
A mount no live mount namespace sees any more is unmounted, as below,
once nothing holds it.
A permanent mount may be taken from the view and stays mounted; the root
of the mount table returns
.Er EINVAL .
.Pp
The mount stops accepting new operations and waits for operations
already in progress to finish.
An open file description, working directory, root directory set by
.Xr chroot 2 ,
program a process runs, or child mount in any process then makes it busy.
A loop device attached to a file on the mount also prevents unmounting.
.Pp
The file system syncs and prepares to unmount; the kernel writes dirty
cache buffers, flushes the device, and drops its cached buffers.
A tmpfs has no device and writes nothing; its unmount frees its files.
For a writable KRFFS volume, preparation flushes earlier writes to the
medium before clearing and separately flushing the dirty flag.
A KRFFS volume made read-only before preparation, or with an open file
that lost its name, as at a shutdown, keeps that flag set; the next
mount frees the file.
Only then does it release the file system, covered directory, and claim.
.Pp
A write that fails does not keep the mount, as on Linux:
.Fn umount2
completes and returns 0, and the kernel logs the failure.
After the file system's sync fails, or on a device whose earlier write
failed, the volume is not prepared and no cached buffer is written;
the device is flushed and the buffers still dirty are dropped.
A writeback or flush that fails after preparation puts back what
preparation wrote.
Either way the volume stays marked for a check: an ext2 volume keeps its
valid bit clear, and a KRFFS, FAT32 or exFAT volume its dirty flag.
.Pp
A FAT32 or exFAT mount made read-only by a failed write can be unmounted
once its references are released.
Unmount does not clear the volume's dirty flag.
.Pp
A mount whose device has been removed can be unmounted once its
references are released, without writes to the absent device.
If a removed ancestor prevents path lookup, the recorded target can
identify a unique active child mount below it.
A caller whose root
.Xr chroot 2
moved names the target from that root, and only a mount below the root
matches.
Children must be unmounted before their parents.
.Ss System call interface
The i386 system call number for
.Fn mount
is 21, with source in EBX, target in ECX, type in EDX, flags in ESI,
and data in EDI.
The number for
.Fn umount2
is 52, with target in EBX and flags in ECX.
On x86-64 the numbers are 165 and 166, with the same arguments in RDI,
RSI, RDX, R10 and R8.
For a program that enters the x86-64 kernel by
.Ql int $0x80 ,
which
.Xr ia32 7
describes, i386's
.Nm umount
(22) is
.Fn umount2
with no flags.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions return 0 on success, or -1 on failure and set
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /proc/mounts
.It Pa /proc/mounts
Active mounts with the flags listed above.
Mounts whose devices have been removed, and the tmpfs of
.Xr memfd_create 2 ,
are omitted.
.El
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ENAMETOOLONG]"
.It Bq Er EACCES
An ancestor directory denies search permission.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
The target is covered, reserved, or in a mount undergoing a transition;
the source overlaps a claimed range or an open raw writer;
the source device is already mounted; an unmount is prevented by
references; or a remount read-only finds a description open for writing
or another such remount of the mount under way.
Unmounting a permanent mount without
.Dv MNT_DETACH
also returns this error.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A required string pointer is null or a string cannot be read from
user memory.
.It Bq Er EFBIG
A KRFFS source device exceeds the size the file system can address.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
A flag is unsupported, the mount target is the root or in a synthetic
namespace, but for the root of a mount and, for a bind, a name in the
root directory of one, a bind's source is
.Pa /proc/PID/fd/N ,
the source of a type with a device is not a supported block
device, the unmount target is not a mount root, or a lazy unmount names
the root of the mount table.
A constructor also returns this error for invalid options or volume
structure it cannot accept.
.It Bq Er EIO
Path lookup or file-system I/O failed, or the backing device was removed.
.It Bq Er ELOOP
Target resolution would follow more than 40 symbolic links.
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
A copied string or joined path exceeds its buffer, the mount target
exceeds 255 bytes, or a path component or symbolic-link expansion is
too long.
.It Bq Er ENODEV
The file-system type is unknown.
The type
.Ql sysfs
is unknown to
.Fn mount :
the kernel makes the one
.Pa /sys
at boot.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
A target component does not exist, or the source device is not
published.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
Memory for path resolution, a mount record, or file-system state is
unavailable, or the sector-claim table is full.
.It Bq Er ENOTDIR
The mount target or a directory component is not a directory, or the
unmount target ends in a slash or in
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&..
and reaches something else.
.It Bq Er ENXIO
The source is a loop device with no file attached, whose attachment is still
being made, or whose file\(aqs device has gone.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The caller's effective user ID is not 0, and it is not privileged over its
own mount namespace, or is and mounts a type other than
.Ql proc ,
.Ql sysfs ,
.Ql tmpfs ,
.Ql mqueue
and
.Ql devpts ,
or remounts a file system.
A caller privileged over its own mount namespace may also make a bind,
change propagation and unmount; its mount of
.Ql sysfs
passes this check and fails with
.Er ENODEV .
Such a caller may remount with
.Dv MS_BIND
only a mount that no other live mount namespace sees, and only to add
.Dv MS_RDONLY ,
.Dv MS_NOSUID ,
.Dv MS_NODEV
and
.Dv MS_NOEXEC ,
never to clear one the mount has, as Linux locks the flags of the mounts
a user namespace inherits.
.It Bq Er EROFS
A remount tried to make writable a file system whose device refuses
writes or has failed a write, an ext2 volume mounted read-only for a
feature the driver does not write.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
There is no current process.
.El
